def main():
    aux=[]
    def add(n,s,ps,note,deps=()):aux.append(dict(local_id='A'+str(n),statement_original=s,evidence=[dict(page=p,location='Original main-text context A'+str(n)) for p in ps],scope_note=note,depends_on=list(deps)))
    add(1,r'''For each client $k$, the observations $\mathcal D_k=\{\boldsymbol\xi_t^k\}_{t=1}^{n_k}$ are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.), drawn from $P_k$, and $n_k$ is the local sample size. The full dataset of all clients is $\mathcal D=\bigcup_{k=1}^K\mathcal D_k$, leading to the overall sample size $N=\sum_{k=1}^Kn_k$.''',[3],'Original within-client sampling statement. It does not explicitly spell out a cross-client joint law.',['D1'])
    add(2,r'''Throughout this paper, when we refer to an estimator of $\boldsymbol\theta_K^*$ as asymptotically efficient, we imply that the estimator possesses the same asymptotic variance as the estimator
\[
\hat{\boldsymbol\theta}_K=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol\theta\in\boldsymbol\Phi}\sum_{k=1}^Kw_k\left(\frac1T\sum_{t=1}^Tf_k(\boldsymbol\theta;\boldsymbol\xi_t^k)\right).\tag{4}
\]''',[3],'Original full-sample M-estimation benchmark, not the actual decentralized algorithm.',['D1','D2','D4','D5'])
    add(3,r'''This condition is sufficient and necessary to ensure $\lim_{s\to\infty}\boldsymbol C^s=K^{-1}\boldsymbol1_K\boldsymbol1_K^T$. Specifically, the case of $\rho=0$ corresponds to the centralized FL scenario with $\boldsymbol C=K^{-1}\boldsymbol1_K\boldsymbol1_K^T$.''',[6],'Original K-dimensional averaging expression, retained beside the d-dimensional J notation.',['D9','D10'])
    add(4,r'''Thus, this theorem informs that there exists a universal constant $c_0$ such that the consensus error in the DFL algorithm $K^{-1}\mathbb E(\|\hat{\boldsymbol\Theta}_t(\boldsymbol I-\boldsymbol J)\|_F^2)\le c_0\eta_t^2$ for all $1\le t\le T$.''',[9],'Original simplified rate that binds c0 in Theorem 2. It is a stated main-text consequence, not permission to import the full proof as hypotheses.',['D8','D11','D21'])
    add(5,r'''If $D>2/\mu_{R_d}$ and $\gamma>0$ such that $\eta_1\le D/(D\mu_{R_d}-1)$, and let $c_0$ be a positive constant such that $K^{-1}\mathbb E(\|\hat{\boldsymbol\Theta}_t(\boldsymbol I-\boldsymbol J)\|_F^2)\le c_0\eta_t^2$ as implied in Theorem 1''',[10],'Conditional hypothesis of Theorem 2(ii), kept in full as context for later references to assumptions of Theorem 2. Those later references do not specify the subpart. Do not turn the fixed-K conclusion of (iii) into a common hypothesis.',['D8','D11','D21','D23'])
    add(6,r'''We begin by noting that
\[
\boldsymbol S=K\sum_{k=1}^Kw_k^2\mathbb E(\boldsymbol\epsilon_k(\boldsymbol\theta_K^*;\boldsymbol\xi^k)\boldsymbol\epsilon_k(\boldsymbol\theta_K^*;\boldsymbol\xi^k)^T),
\]''',[12],'Original no-cross-term covariance expression. Its relation to the stated within-client sampling is recorded as a joint-law clarification issue.',['D2','D17','D25','D28'])
    add(7,r'''The second term in (22) is necessary in the expression of $\hat{\boldsymbol S}_k$ since generally $\nabla F_k(\boldsymbol\theta_K^*)\ne\boldsymbol0_{d\times1}$ due to the heterogeneity across the clients.''',[13],'Original reason for centering the client gradient covariance.',['D1','D4','D29'])
    add(8,r'''where $\chi^2_{d,\beta}$ is the upper $\beta$ quantile of the $\chi^2_d$ distribution''',[14],'Original quantile convention for Theorem 4 and its confidence region.',['D33'])
    add(9,r'''where $\tilde{\boldsymbol\Sigma}=(\tilde{\boldsymbol H}^{\mathrm{reg}})^{-1}\hat{\boldsymbol S}(\tilde{\boldsymbol H}^{\mathrm{reg}})^{-1}$, and $\hat{\boldsymbol S}$ is defined in (23).''',[16],'Original proposed nonsmooth-loss sandwich, with the inverse repeated without a transpose. It is downstream context rather than the claim in Theorem 5.',['D30','D38'])
    bindings={
    'T1':'Resolve the assumptions cited through Lemma 1: network contraction, polynomial step sizes, gradient-noise moments with v=1, convex L-smooth risks and gradient heterogeneity. Lemma 1 supplies B_MSE and B_CE used in the theorem-local Q definition. Keep both parameter-domain branches, all three delta terms, the transformed rho and the finite t/tau range.',
    'T2':'Resolve the common numbered assumptions, spatial mean, target, matrix disagreement and derived curvature mu_Rd. Delta_t, c1/c2 and v1/v2 are bound inside the statement. Part (ii) has its own D/gamma/c0 hypotheses; part (iii) alone fixes K for almost sure convergence. Theorem 1 supplies the definition/context of c0, not a new assumption on the data beyond the stated rate.',
    'T3':'Resolve the common assumptions referenced from Theorem 2 and the explicit Assumptions 4.1–4.3, plus the joint PR average, H and S. Keep finite K or K=o(T^(2alpha-1)), alpha<1 and the uniform target bound. Later references to Theorem 2 leave the scope of its conditional part-(ii) step-size restrictions unstated; preserve that source context and mark the scope ambiguity, rather than rewriting the theorem. Do not impose fixed K.',
    'T4':'Resolve the same base assumptions, with stochastic-Hessian Assumption 4.4 replacing the population-Hessian Assumption 4.3. The theorem uses centered covariance estimates, the trailing-window smooth Hessian estimate and the resulting sandwich. Keep K*a(T)->infinity, both uniform target/gradient bounds and the upper-beta chi-square convention. Do not require each client Hessian estimate to be individually consistent.',
    'T5':'Use the printed list: network and step-size assumptions, noise moments with v=2, heterogeneity/curvature, stochastic-gradient smoothness and population-Hessian regularity. Preserve bounded Phi for both alpha regimes, the two client-growth rates and the thresholded regression Hessian. Theorem 5 does not list Assumption 3.2 or require stochastic losses to have Hessians. Its conclusion is convergence of expected Frobenius norm, not squared norm.',
    'T6':'Resolve the common Theorem 2 assumptions and explicit 4.1,4.2,4.4, but use alpha=1 and the corrected PR estimator in (33). Its gradient correction is averaged over local prior iterates and uses the smooth plug-in Hessian. Preserve uppercase F_k with a data argument in the printed expansion and the unresolved notation discrepancy. The final CLT requires K and T both diverging, K=o(T), and both displayed remainder terms.'}
